      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the properties of remainders when dividing integers. \n\nLet's denote the integer by \\( n \\).\n\nAccording to the given information, when \\( n \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7. This is expressed as:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is an integer.\n\nNow, we need to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. To find these remainders, we can substitute \\( n = 15k + 7 \\) into the expressions for the remainders.\n\nFirst, let's find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nWe can rewrite \\( 15k \\) as \\( 3(5k) \\), so:\n\\[ n = 3(5k) + 7 \\]\nSince the factor of 3 is already a multiple of 3, it will not affect the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3. Hence, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 will be the same as the remainder when \\( 7 \\) is divided by 3. The remainder when \\( 7 \\) is divided by 3 is \\( 1 \\). Thus, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is \\( 1 \\).\n\nNext, let's find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nWe can rewrite \\( 15k \\) as \\( 5(3k) \\), so:\n\\[ n = 5(3k) + 7 \\]\nSince the factor of 5 is already a multiple of 5, it will not affect the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5. Hence, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 will be the same as the remainder when \\( 7 \\) is divided by 5. The remainder when \\( 7 \\) is divided by 5 is \\( 2 \\). Thus, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is \\( 2 \\).\n\nFinally, the sum of the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5 is:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nSo, the sum of the remainders is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
